  {
    "episode_id": 75275,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75275.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely not. I had no idea what I was getting into. I was one of about 1200 people that showed up and took the test at Hollywood High School. And 26 of us were hired back in 1991. The city had stopped hiring 911 dispatchers because of budgetary constraints. And our class was the first class that they hired in about three or four years. I had no idea, though. It wasn't until orientation that I realized what I was getting into when I was giving not only the police manual, but also the department manual. And I was told that it would be a lengthy process of studying both of those and learning about local, state, and federal laws in order to do my job. I think that that is something that people just really don't understand how much training goes into doing this job. And as normal citizens going around about our business, the first time we dial 911 is oftentimes not the most positive time in our life. We're not calling because we're having a great day. We're calling because there is something very serious happening. And all of us want to make sure that we have the best trained person answering our call.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
