    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75275.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely. I talked about in the letter, I talk about the training aspect of the work. I talk about the different types of testing and that they have to go through. I talk about their training and their ongoing training, I should say, because it's not the type of job that you can be trained for once and there. And I talk about You know, the different types of work that they have to do, for example, they are often the negotiators in a crime scene initially. They talk to suspects specifically when it comes to domestic violence cases where, you know, one person gets shot. The suspect is usually the person calling And they are Miranda and 911 dispatchers are Miranda exempt. I was at a Homeland Security meeting where I talked to the FBI director and said, you have to include 911 dispatchers in your training, the training that you do and provide for our first responders. They are talking to suspects, they are talking to victims of crime, and the more information they are able to draw you know, the more information that you will have available to identify potential terrorists, you know, in our community. And therefore, this letter, I think, really talks about that very unique work that they do. And then, you know, from my perspective, it puts a personal experience to the job And I hope that with that attention that OMB will do the right thing and not deny 911 dispatchers what they deserve.",
